{
  "configuration_id": "config_006",
  "reason": "Evidence: All three completed experiments use singleprofile. RNA-only (scgpt) achieves 0.411 acc, image-only (ViT-L) is weak at 0.227, and multimodal concat (scgpt+ViT-L) reaches 0.435 acc / 0.423 F1 — a modest but consistent gain, especially in F1. The largest untested design dimension is the profile setting (single vs. multiprofile), which also introduces the aggregation choice. Question: Does moving from singleprofile to multiprofile (with the simplest aggregation, avgpool) improve performance while keeping the same encoders (scgpt, imagenet_vit_l) and fusion (concat) as the best result (config_049)? This isolates the profile/aggregation effect with minimal confounding. Why this is the best next experiment: config_006 changes only the profile setting (and adds the simplest aggregation) relative to the current best, making it a clean controlled comparison. If multiprofile+avgpool helps, we gain a new promising axis and can later test transformer aggregation; if it does not, we rule out profile multiplicity as a key lever and redirect budget toward encoder or fusion choices. Under a limited budget, resolving this high-uncertainty, high-impact design question with a single-variable change is more informative than jumping to a more complex configuration (e.g., transformer aggregation or a different encoder) that would confound multiple factors."
}

